&lt;p&gt;Join us for a virtual screening of &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Cabaret Total&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. Watch the film at home, then join us for a discussion with &lt;strong&gt;Roy Assaf&lt;/strong&gt;, writer, director, and star of the film.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Monday, October 26, at noon | Zoom&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;em&gt;*Registered guests will receive an email on Friday, October 23 with a link to watch the film. The film will be available to view at any time through Monday, October 26.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;A separate email with the Zoom link to the Q&amp;amp;A will be sent the morning of Monday, October 26, ahead of the discussion at noon.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Acclaimed Israeli actor &lt;strong&gt;Roy Assaf&lt;/strong&gt; (Ophir Award winner for Wounded Land and God&amp;#39;s Neighbors) co-writes and stars in his feature directing debut about a high school drama teacher who stages vivid cabaret shows in his Negev town&amp;rsquo;s community center. When a comment to his students makes him a target of social media rage, his art and life merge in escalating performances of 21st-Century anxiety.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Assaf sings, dances, juggles, and monologues in a virtuosic performance, shot with Ophir-Award-winning cinematography reminiscent of the Oscar-winning Birdman. Frequent attendees at IIJS film events will recognize Roy Assaf from his films Matchmaking, All I Can Do, and Wedding Doll.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;(90 minutes; Hebrew with English subtitles)&lt;/p&gt;
