&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speaker: &lt;/strong&gt;Dr. &lt;a href="https://zongyi-li.github.io/" target="_blank"&gt;Zongyi Li&lt;/a&gt;, NYU&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Title:&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;quot;Generative Models for PDEs: Building, Scaling, and Evaluation&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Abstract: &lt;/strong&gt;Generative models for partial differential equations must capture both short-term dynamics and statistical behavior beyond the predictability horizon. This talk connects two challenges: building models with these capabilities and evaluating their fidelity. I will introduce GenNO, a flow-matching transformer pretrained across 24 physical systems that supports prediction, reconstruction, and adaptation with limited data. Scaling improves its statistical fidelity even when pointwise accuracy saturates. I will then present PDE-KD, a sample-efficient distributional metric that evaluates simulations after predicted and reference trajectories decorrelate. Together, these approaches provide a framework for learning and assessing transferable physical simulators.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Bio:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;Zongyi Li is an Assistant Professor of Mathematics and Data Science at New York University, affiliated with the Courant Institute and the Center for Data Science. His research focuses on machine learning for scientific computing, particularly neural operators and foundation models for physical systems. Previously, he was a Postdoctoral Associate at MIT, working with Kaiming He. He received his Ph.D. from Caltech, advised by Anima Anandkumar and Andrew Stuart, and his B.S. from Washington University in St. Louis. He is an AI2050 Schmidt Sciences Early Career Fellow.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr /&gt;
