&lt;p&gt;A 3&amp;deg;C temperature spike in the Eastern Pacific Ocean is changing weather patterns around the planet. This climate phenomenon, El Ni&amp;ntilde;o, brings more frequent and intense heatwaves, greater drought and wildfire risk, and heavier rainfall. El Ni&amp;ntilde;o can affect nearly every part of daily life and industry, from the food we eat to how we travel.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Fortunately, there are steps society can take to prepare for these disruptions. On October 27, the Columbia Climate School invites you to an exclusive webinar, where you can ask the questions the headlines don&amp;rsquo;t answer. Get the science behind the forecasts, find out what it means for your life and work, and learn what to expect in the months ahead, straight from our panel of climate experts.&lt;/p&gt;
