&lt;p&gt;Join us for a conversation about Emily Feng&amp;rsquo;s new book, &lt;a href="https://globalreports.columbia.edu/books/our-hearts-burned-for-home" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;OUR HEARTS BURNED FOR HOME: Uyghur Militants in Syria&amp;rsquo;s Civil War&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, published this month by Columbia Global Reports.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Currently a White House correspondent for NPR, Feng spent many years as an international correspondent for the network, covering China, Taiwan, Southeast Asia, and conflict zones in the Middle East. She and WSJ&amp;rsquo;s Natasha Khan will discuss how Feng came to write her new book&amp;mdash;the first to feature in-person, in-depth interviews with the Uyghur militant community in Syria; how her years of reporting in the region helped her make the necessary connections to the Uyghurs featured in the book; and what she hopes her work&amp;mdash;both this book and her journalism&amp;mdash;will illuminate for the public about the little-understood Uyghur community, and other refugee or exile groups.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This talk is open to the campus community and the public. Registration is required at the following link:&lt;a href="https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://docs.google.com/forms/d/1oqNJpt6LC34RmKoLzcWQoNLCc4_g0qhZb852MvOhZVc/edit__;!!BDUfV1Et5lrpZQ!SrepIVNA6tkyS5UMbcIF9UX_h5UDyfvMzx5hbByYCkm5_HDGmm-YZzaSbUcoeqBr3Zn3PzkvCCnHWa0o0D5z8pS8$" target="_blank"&gt; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href="https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://forms.gle/GPUupQxYRXbjPrhf8__;!!BDUfV1Et5lrpZQ!XW3XqSeKWlyJZUiMJ205iCAQt55PwgBJLc6JspNkGTpyVTlDstW-CeA3sPDZUTL3ZSRVlLLIqgBg8IOT8APrpWwX$" target="_blank"&gt;https://forms.gle/GPUupQxYRXbjPrhf8&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Registration is required by 11:59 PM on Monday, October 26.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Copies of Feng&amp;rsquo;s book will be available for sale at the event.&lt;/p&gt;

