&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speaker: &lt;/strong&gt;Dr. &lt;a href="https://www.lanl.gov/about/leadership-governance/patrick-fitch/mark-b-chadwick" target="_blank"&gt;Mark B. Chadwick&lt;/a&gt;, Los Alamos National Lab&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Title: &lt;/strong&gt;&amp;quot;Fusion&amp;rsquo;s Surprising History--and Its Unfinished Future&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Abstract:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An overlooked experiment, a remarkably favorable nuclear resonance, and the challenge of controlling a burning plasma: fusion&amp;rsquo;s history offers lessons for its future. This talk traces that history from early ideas about the energy source of stars and a little-known 1938 observation of deuterium&amp;ndash;tritium fusion at the University of Michigan to discoveries at Los Alamos that helped establish the foundations of fusion research.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Drawing on recent historical investigations, I will describe how experiments revealed the unusually large deuterium&amp;ndash;tritium fusion cross section, why its underlying nuclear resonance matters, and how precision measurements and nuclear theory continue to underpin fusion predictions. I will then connect these foundations to contemporary challenges: plasma disruptions, neutron damage to materials, predictive simulation, and emerging uses of artificial intelligence. Developments in magnetic confinement, inertial confinement, and pulsed-power fusion provide the setting for discussing the opportunities&amp;mdash;and substantial remaining challenges&amp;mdash;of commercial fusion energy.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Throughout, the emphasis will be on scientific surprises, the interplay of experiment and computation, and the open problems that the next generation of researchers can help solve.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Bio:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Dr. Mark B. Chadwick is the Associate Laboratory Director for Simulation, Computing &amp;amp; Theory (ALDSCT) at Los Alamos, advancing the high-performance computing, algorithms, and theoretical science that underpin national security missions and AI and quantum discovery science. He previously served as interim Los Alamos Deputy Director for Science, Technology &amp;amp; Engineering. Chadwick has worked across Los Alamos&amp;rsquo; Science, Technology &amp;amp; Engineering and Weapons organizations for more than three decades. He has led the US nuclear data evaluation collaboration Evaluated Nuclear Data Files, creating the world&amp;rsquo;s highest-fidelity databases used worldwide in radiation transport codes, including the Laboratory&amp;rsquo;s Monte Carlo N-Particle code.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Chadwick has also published technical nuclear history books on fission and fusion during the Manhattan Project and H-bomb invention era. He is a Los Alamos Laboratory Fellow, and an APS and ANS Fellow.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;----&lt;/p&gt;
