&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLScxI0-CEWb4syFQfVcifpR0v1UgMdYjmE3YZgDIasDP4P8XLQ/viewform" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;RSVP HERE&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A guest lecture with &lt;strong&gt;Monique Allewaert&lt;/strong&gt;, Professor of English at University of Wisconsin-Madison.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This talk introduces the letters a free black woman and an enslaved man penned for a colonial projector in eighteenth-century Saint Domingue (Haiti). Ostensibly vehicles for colonial natural history, these letters also spark with residues of the labor, knowledges, and experiences of the racialized writers who scribed them. The talk foregrounds moments where the letters describe the West Indian landscape. By reading these passages, as well as the materiality of the letters, against their &amp;quot;bias grain&amp;quot; (Marisa Fuentes), I seek to turn description-once the colonial naturalist&amp;#39;s tool for manufacturing a univocal nature-into an instrument of decolonial placemaking. This method of (re)description feeds on the letters&amp;#39; own energies to disclose para-sites: places and powers surviving at the edges of plantation enclosure.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Presented by&amp;nbsp;The Early Americanist and 18th-Century British Colloquia. Co-sponsored by the Center for American Studies, the Institute for the Study of Gender &amp;amp; Sexuality, and the Department of English and Comparative Literature at Columbia University.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What happens when friendship, ambition, and cultural identity take center stage?&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Join the &lt;a href="https://universitylife.columbia.edu/graduate-initiative"&gt;Graduative Initiative&lt;/a&gt; for a night on Broadway to see &lt;a href="https://www.manhattantheatreclub.com/"&gt;Manhattan Theatre Club&amp;rsquo;s&lt;/a&gt; production of &lt;a href="https://www.manhattantheatreclub.com/shows/2026-27-season/school-girls-or-the-african-mean-girls-play/"&gt;&lt;em&gt;School Girls; Or, The African Mean Girls Play&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, a sharp, funny, and thought-provoking comedy that explores friendship, cultural identity, beauty standards, and belonging within a Ghanaian girl&amp;rsquo;s boarding school.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We&amp;rsquo;ll kick off the evening on campus with a light meal, good conversation, and a little community-building around the play&amp;rsquo;s themes. Then, we&amp;rsquo;ll head downtown for a night of theater, culture, laughter, and new perspectives.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Take a break from the usual grad-school routine and experience New York City&amp;rsquo;s vibrant arts and cultural scene with your fellow grad students. Come connect, explore, laugh and enjoy a night on Broadway!&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
