&lt;p&gt;Join us for a talk with Benjamin Lewis Robinson (New York University).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About the Event:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Three recent emergencies&amp;mdash;the so-called European migrant crisis, the climate emergency, and the COVID-19 pandemic&amp;mdash;confirm the impression that modern politics is emergency politics. They also revealed its treacherous shortcomings. The emergency measures taken are inevitably short-term, stop-gap, presentist, parochial, and anthropocentric. Reproducing a dangerously reductive framing of the political scene, the politics of emergency obscures the underlying social, biological, infrastructural, and ecological conditions of need that give rise to emergencies. Engaging the thought of Hannah Arendt and Giorgio Agamben, Robinson will argue that doing justice to the contemporary scene must begin not top-down with sovereign action but bottom-up with needs. Modern politics is not exhausted in &amp;ldquo;states of emergency&amp;rdquo; but must be understood as navigating the irreducible conjunction of need and emergency. Arendt and Agamben share what Robinson calls the dramatic prejudice of political theory. It perpetuates a view of politics as a scene focused on action and actors; agitations that take the form of need are politically insignificant. In contrast, Robinson argues that such agitations precede the political drama and exceed the scope of action. It is the play of exigencies that first provokes the attention of agents and agencies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About the Speaker:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Benjamin Lewis Robinson&lt;/strong&gt; is Assistant Professor of German at NYU. His first book, Bureaucratic Fanatics: Modern Literature and the Passions of Rationalization (2019), treats the literary engagement with the bureaucratic transformation of life in the long nineteenth century. A second book Need/Emergency: Political Theater in Exigent Times will come out with Stanford University Press in 2026. In critical engagement with Arendt, who considered theater to be the political art par excellence, it treats works of theater by Jelinek, B&amp;uuml;chner, Kleist, and H&amp;ouml;lderlin that in different ways challenge theoretical views on the modern political scene. He is currently working on a book on J. M. Coetzee titled Just Fiction. Ben is co-editor of a special issue of The Germanic Review titled &amp;ldquo;Schuld (guilt/debt) in the Anthropocene&amp;rdquo; and of the volume The Work of World Literature (2021).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The talk is followed by a reception. RSVP Requested.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Richard Gottfried&lt;/strong&gt;, the longest-serving NYS assemblyman in history, will reflect on his five decades of playing a pivotal role in shaping health policy and on the increasingly important role of states in this area.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Speaker:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Richard (&amp;quot;Dick&amp;quot;) Gottfried&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;was first elected to the NYS Assembly in 1970 while he was still a student at Columbia Law School. He served as the Assemblymember for Manhattan&amp;#39;s West Side for over 50 years, the longest serving Assemblymember in New York history.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Assemblymember Gottfried has had an enormous influence on legislation and regulations of interest to bioethics professionals. To name a few examples, he was the key sponsor of:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The Family Health Care Decisions Act, which authorized surrogate decision-making for patients who lack capacity;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The Palliative Care Education and Training Act and Palliative Care Information Act&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;NY&amp;#39;s Medical Marijuana Law&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The Medical Aid in Dying Act, which was enacted a few years after he retired&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;He also sponsored bills on among other bioethical issues, DNR orders, health care proxies, anatomical gifts, human subjects research, confidentiality, and more.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;And notably, he fought passionately&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;for many years for a New York single payer universal health care law &amp;ndash; the New York Health Act. That fight goes on.&lt;/p&gt;
